## Visual Appearance
Unlike the liquid-metal, shifting frame of the military-grade Kestrel, the Torrent-8 is built with practical utility in mind. It has a rigid, blocky, and highly durable frame made of recycled carbon-composite plastics.
It still lacks wheels, utilizing a basic Ground-Effect Mag-Lev disc at the front and back, which emits a noticeable amber glow and a low, mechanical drone sound. Instead of invisible cloaking armor, it features a rugged, scuffed matte-industrial paint job with exposed wiring bundles and mechanical access panels for easy repairs.
## Propulsion and Mechanics

* Hydrogen-Fuel Core: It does not possess an infinite power supply. It runs on standard, commercially available solid-state hydrogen cells that require swapping or recharging every 600 kilometers.
* Single-Mode Mobility: The Torrent-8 cannot fly. It is hard-locked by mandatory civilian firmware to a Ground-Sweep Mode only. It hovers at a strict, safe altitude of exactly 1 meter above the ground or water. It can travel over rough terrain or water, but it cannot scale vertical cliffs or lift into the sky.
* Top Speed: Maxes out at a structurally governed 180 km/h. It lacks protective force fields, so it features a physical, curved plexiglass windshield to block oncoming bugs, dust, and wind.

## Tactical & Civilian Features

* Manual Handlebar Controls: There is no telepathic neural-link. The rider steers using traditional physical handlebars equipped with dual-axis twist grips and a basic holographic heads-up display (HUD) projected onto the windshield showing speed, navigation, and fuel levels.
* Magnetic Cargo Racks: In place of weapon pods or military drone bays, the rear chassis is fitted with heavy-duty electromagnetic cargo plates. Courier drivers and wasteland scavengers use these to snap-lock storage crates, survival gear, or passenger seats directly onto the bike frame.
* Physical Proximity Alarms: It features no combat shields. Instead, it relies on basic commercial radar sensors that automatically pulse the brakes or steer the bike away if the rider gets too close to a wall, tree, or another vehicle.

